// test/tty_test.py drives this program second. It writes what
// test/tty/print_then_wait.ft writes, but through std.rt's entry points
// instead of the print family. The two programs then answer D11.5's
// interactive question side by side: on a terminal the runtime flushes each
// line as the program writes it, and on a pipe stdout waits while stderr does
// not. The compiler still lowers a print to the C runtime, so one program
// cannot do both jobs.
//
// This program calls flush_all itself at the end. The main the compiler emits
// flushes the C runtime's buffers and not these. That call is the only
// difference from the program beside it, and it runs after the parent has
// read everything it asks about.
import std.io;
import std.rt;

fn i32 main() {
    rt.print_str(1, "out\n".ptr, 4);
    rt.print_str(2, "err\n".ptr, 4);
    // This blocks until the parent sends a byte. Nothing below it runs, and
    // nothing flushes, while the parent reads what arrived.
    u8[1] mut gate = {};
    io.read(io.STDIN, gate[..]);
    rt.print_str(1, "bye\n".ptr, 4);
    // This blocks a second time, so the parent reads the line above while the
    // process still runs. Closing the last slave of a pty drops what is still
    // in its buffer, so a line read after the exit would be a race.
    io.read(io.STDIN, gate[..]);
    rt.flush_all();
    return 0;
}
